Hi, all!

I'm the one who posted that "We don't need no steenkin' reflection!"
article a few days ago, and I was delighted at the response.  Quite a
few people e-mailed me asking to hear the game's outcome.  It just 
ended, so I will be glad to post a summary.  Here goes:

Sierra-S ascended to demigodhood with 5,986,551 points!  Damn...missed
that six million mark!  ;) 
 During the game (you are _not_ going to believe this!)
1:  I never used any reflection item.
2:  I never died (i.e. never used my amulet of life saving).
3:  I never bothered to engrave Elbereth.
4:  I #prayed a total of four times (3 of these were for holy water).
I do not use the save/restore method of playing, so this was a "real" win.
This was also the first time I have won NetHack 3.1, though I won 3.0 many
times.

WARNING:  The levels of luck in the following game may make you hurl!

The game went something like this:
Started off as a Samurai (my favorite), and found a luckstone on the first
level.  Went down and found shops on the next three levels...and proceeded
to crack all three (with help from my dog).  The dog fetched a +5 (!) Cloak
of Protection out of the armor shop on level two.  (GOOD doggie!)  I never
bothered to pick up another cloak.  The dog also stole a ring of invisibility
for me out of the next shop.  After that, I took a pickaxe to a few vaults
and went down to the Gnome Mines.  Lost my dog to a pit trap on level 4.  :(
At town level, I #offered two wands of create monster worth of corpses at
the co-aligned altar.  Also donated all that vault gold to the priest.  Got
Snickersnee (a fine weapon!) on the second sacrifice.  :)  Took all the 
crappy potions I had been finding and turned them into a lot of holy water
(I think I had 14 potions of holy water at one point).  I used them to bless
some useful items, including a tinning kit and a Bag of Holding.  

I didn't bother to go below the town level, but just went back to the Dungeons
of Doom and started heading down.  I ran into a pair of co-aligned unicorns
on level 7 and tossed most of my gems to them.  With that blessed luckstone,
my luck stayed at the extremely lucky level until I reached the Astral Plane
and had to kill all those Angels...

The next big find was a ring of conflict, immediately followed by a magic lamp!
The wish got used for a ring of teleport control, as I had stupidly eaten a
nymph corpse and I find random teleporting extremely annoying.  (This after
four tengu corpses failed to give me teleport control.)  

Then, I went on the quest.  I ate a tin of stalker meat while invisible to 
make myself permanently invisible and this also gave me the essential see 
invisible intrinsic.  I wasn't high enough level (only 12th) at that point to
tackle the quest, so I decided to take a break and clear out the rest of the
Gnome mines.  Was that ever a good move!  On the mine levels below the town,
I found FOUR potions of gain ability!  The first one (randomly quaffing an
unknown potion) was blessed, and I had enough holy water to bless the other 
three before I drank them.  Hello, awesome stats!  :)  Then I hit a polymorph
trap and (randomly) turned into a succubus.  These are excellent thieves, by
the way.  After I reverted to human form, and just when I was thinking things
couldn't get any better, I find another magic lamp in the mines!  Hello +3
GDSM!  (My usual first wish when I've already got a good weapon)  

Out of the mines and back down...  I went all the way to the castle without
anything really unusual happening.  As I was finishing off the last few 
monsters in the castle, I finally reached 13th level.  ...and I needed to
be 14th to go on the quest.  Got the WoW, first wish was for a blessed 
scroll of charging, of course.  After using most of the wishes up, I decided
to "waste" one of them.  I didn't have fire resistance at that point, so
I really didn't want to go beyond the castle, and I had cleared all the other
levels available.  So, how am I going to get the extra level I need for the
Quest?  I decided to wish for a blessed potion of gain level.  No, wait...
if I ask for more than one, and don't get it, it will still give me a potion
anyway, right?  So, I decided to gamble and asked for "4 blessed potions of
gain ability"  *poof*  n - 4 potions of gain ability.  I LOVE it when I get
what I ask for!  :)  After the wish-fest from the castle, I was AC -24,
with speed boots, helm of telepathy, etc., etc. I also was resistant to just 
about everything.

After the castle, the Quest was trivial.  Once I got my hands on The Tsurugi
of Muramasa, I had everything I needed.  The Tsurugi is _the_ best weapon in
the game.  It never misses, and it is at least as destructive as Mjollnir,
plus it has this wonderful instant kill power:  "The razor sharp blade slices
the Wizard of Yendor in half!" --more--  (I got that glorious message *twice*
both times when the Wiz had just appeared and was at full hit points!)
I picked up the last intrinsic I needed (fire resistance) from a red dragon
corpse and procceded to Gehennom.

I spent a long time in Gehennom.  I ate enough wraiths and Giant Zombies to
max out both my strength and my level.  (When I ascended, all my stats were
18 (or 18/**) except for a 15 charisma.)  I had one hell of a time finding
Vlad's tower.  I didn't think the entrance could be on a special level, but
it was!  Once I cleaned that out, I went to the Wizard's tower, killed him, 
and got the Book.  I then went down to the bottom (I had found the vibrating
spot ahead of time) went to Moloch's lair, got the amulet, and then went up.
After I got the Book, my character was so powerful, and so hard to hit, that
I was never really challenged...my hit points never went below 300 and my max
at ascension was 375.  Because of that long time in Gehennom, and a blessed 
spellbook of identify, I had four screens worth of valuable gems in the BoH
when I ascended.  I also used a charm monster spell to pick up a pet Archon
on the astral plane.  Hey, why not?

So that's the tale.  Are you still awake?  :)

The reason I never reflected anything is simple:  The Tsurugi is a two 
handed weapon (no shield), and I was wearing an amulet of life saving.
I _did_ have an amulet of reflection, but it never got used.  Once your
armor class gets to be -20 or better, and once you have all the resistances,
you really do not need reflection.  On lower levels, especially before you
get sleep resistance, it is a whole different story...  And, yes, I will
admit to getting an obscenely huge run of good luck this game.  I got some
very nice items and many useful intrinsics early, and that made all the 
difference.

Please, don't quote all of this if you reply...
